Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Riemannian Infrageometry", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Riemannian infrageometry measures and describes a discrete substrate \
represented by a graph ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["G", "TI"], "\[LongEqual]", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{
      StyleBox["V", "TI"], ",", 
      StyleBox["E", "TI"]}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1181adb3-ba16-46d0-9f48-8572bf3b129b"],
 ". Euclidean infrageometry, in contrast, is synthetic geometry: \
constructions with natural objects (points, segments, lines, circles) in the \
substrate."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "GuideFunctionsSection",
 CellID->1866139230,ExpressionUUID->"a0ea3657-4c83-4862-93c8-ecf80cebc774"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Classical geometry has infinitesimality: a segment can be subdivided \
infinitely many times, whatever the scale of the observer. A graph has no \
infinitely small part. Infrageometry aggregates instead. An observer scale ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["r", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "ef902b5d-260b-484f-99e4-a171ac20770a"],
 ", the number of steps the observer inspects at once, must be given before \
an object or a measurement makes sense."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"2f744544-8df6-4293-beea-f6527502a7ee"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The claim of infrageometry is that limits of graphs are enough to capture \
the geometry of a surface, although a graph describes only points and a web \
of paths between them. This is close to Riemann's original approach. Riemann \
looked for the infinitesimal object that captures the essence of measuring \
the length of a curve, and found the line element ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
    SuperscriptBox[
     StyleBox["s", "TI"], "2"], "\[LongEqual]", 
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["g", "TI"], 
     StyleBox[
      RowBox[{"i", "j"}], "TI"]], 
    StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
    SuperscriptBox[
     StyleBox["x", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["i", "TI"]], 
    StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
    SuperscriptBox[
     StyleBox["x", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["j", "TI"]]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "81b84cfe-def4-439a-9ccb-f4d6dd2c8ed6"],
 "."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"9b2789db-63eb-442c-96b6-ad62bdfedf8f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In synthetic geometry on a graph a construction is not unique; it is \
multi-valued. Two vertices are in general joined by many shortest paths, and \
so they have many midpoints. We deal with this by enumerating the objects a \
construction gives (points in dimension 0, paths in dimension 1) and \
attaching to the construction its vertex density ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[Rho]", ":", 
    StyleBox["V", "TI"], "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"0", ",", "1", ",", "2", ",", "\[Ellipsis]"}], "}"}]}], 
   TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "68706531-838a-4467-99b1-892ff0e96a39"],
 ", where ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[Rho]", "(", 
    StyleBox["v", "TI"], ")"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "579c2773-fd3e-4ff3-af3a-58ca28066f28"],
 " is the number of objects through the vertex ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["v", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3752d051-444b-4303-acaa-8810b4ce7a94"],
 ". Let graphs converge to a space, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox[
       StyleBox["V", "TI"], 
       StyleBox["n", "TI"]], ",", 
      SubscriptBox[
       StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
       StyleBox["n", "TI"]], ",", 
      SubscriptBox["\[Mu]", 
       StyleBox["n", "TI"]]}], ")"}], "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{
      StyleBox["M", "TI"], ",", 
      StyleBox["d", "TI"], ",", "\[Mu]"}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], 
  "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "f39714ba-bf29-42d6-8130-007dd4a27aad"],
 ", in the Gromov-Hausdorff sense for metric measure spaces, with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"]], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "e5b7f11e-9aec-4e28-926c-d7f672922e66"],
 " the rescaled path metric and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Mu]", 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"]], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "7a6a0dfd-2892-4215-a51d-d463cbd684c8"],
 " the rescaled counting measure. We imagine that the normalized densities ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["\[Rho]", 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "/", 
    UnderscriptBox["\[Sum]", 
     StyleBox["v", "TI"],
     LimitsPositioning->True], 
    SubscriptBox["\[Rho]", 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]], 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     StyleBox["v", "TI"], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "c7b30a69-7035-436a-982c-1d3a87de9acd"],
 " then converge weakly to the Dirac density of the corresponding object in \
the infinitesimal world, concentrated on a point or along a curve."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"f81ae0e8-314d-42fc-afc3-c525cd8c82b7"],

Cell["\<\
The Riemannian branch measures the substrate instead of constructing on it. \
Its objects are the level sets of the distance from a vertex: the closed ball \
B_r(c) = {v : d(c, v) <= r} and the shell S_r(c) = {v : d(c, v) = r}, each a \
sorted vertex list; FindInfraShell computes the shell and InfraShell names it \
inside a scene. A geodesic is read at a scale: the observer sees s steps \
back, and a walk is a geodesic at infra-scale s when every window of s \
consecutive vertices together with the next one is a shortest path, so scale \
1 is any walk and scale Infinity is a segment; FindInfraGeodesic grows them, \
and SegmentGraph and SprayGraph hold every geodesic between two vertices, or \
from one vertex, as a directed acyclic graph whose directed paths are exactly \
the geodesics, built from the distance matrix without listing them. The \
measurements are counts: the ball volume V(r) = |B_r|, the shell area A(r) = \
V(r) - V(r - 1) and the tube volume T(s) = |{w : d(w, core) <= s}|, each \
under a chosen convention for its outermost layer. The log-difference \
quotient q(r) = d log V / d log r is affine in r(r + 1) over a middle window \
of radii, and a regression there reads the dimension as the intercept and the \
scalar curvature as the slope, Bishop-Gromov on a graph; the ball and the \
shell are two independent probes of the same pair, and the tube about a \
geodesic reads R + Ric(v, v) along its core. The metric tensor at p is the \
matrix d(p, u) / d(p, v) with u the foot of v on the interval I(p, w): on a \
substrate whose large-scale metric is round it tends to max(0, cos theta), \
the Gram matrix of the unit directions at p, and on a periodic tiling to the \
projection tensor of the polyhedral norm the path metric converges to. The \
Riemann tensor is not here yet. The ball, tube and cone heads and the two \
geodesic graphs are on the Euclidean Infrageometry guide, the covering \
dimension and the ball intersection complex on the Infra Topology guide, the \
tangent and cotangent spaces on the Infra Analysis guide, and the Levi-Civita \
connection on the Infra Fiber Bundles guide.\
\>", "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"2a7cb1e8-4b92-443b-8e61-33b17c27379c"],

